WEBVTT

00:00:00.267 --> 00:00:03.200
Your go to market, run
by agents you can trust.

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:05.767
Most teams can't say that
about even one agent.

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:09.733
By the end you'll know what GTM OS is.
One place for definitions and rules,

00:00:09.733 --> 00:00:12.433
and a person who says yes
before anything leaves.

00:00:12.467 --> 00:00:14.133
Here's the stack most teams run.

00:00:14.133 --> 00:00:18.433
A CRM, an enrichment tool, a
sequencer, and a pile of spreadsheets.

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:20.567
Each keeps its own idea of a good fit,

00:00:20.567 --> 00:00:23.400
so one account is a priority
here and invisible there.

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:25.500
Nobody can say which tool is right.

00:00:25.533 --> 00:00:27.833
Then Leo gives Orbit access on a Friday.

00:00:27.833 --> 00:00:31.067
By Monday, a customer in the middle
of a renewal has a cold email,

00:00:31.067 --> 00:00:33.200
and nobody can say which rule allowed it.

00:00:33.233 --> 00:00:34.633
Another tool won't fix it.

00:00:34.633 --> 00:00:37.700
You need one place that holds
your rules and enforces them.

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:41.100
Agents move fast. Your
rules decide how far.

00:00:41.167 --> 00:00:45.733
That's GTM OS. You write your definitions
once, as signed, versioned config.

00:00:45.733 --> 00:00:49.600
The engines, the logic that scores,
enriches, routes and drafts,

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:51.300
read only the signed version.

00:00:51.367 --> 00:00:55.200
First, one source of truth for who
you sell to: your ideal customer,

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:58.867
your segments, your scoring model.
Your definitions, signed once.

00:00:58.867 --> 00:01:01.333
Change one, and every engine follows.

00:01:01.367 --> 00:01:05.233
Second, agents never act alone on
anything that leaves the building.

00:01:05.233 --> 00:01:05.767
An agent asks.

00:01:05.767 --> 00:01:09.100
A person decides, and never the
proposer or the human behind it.

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:11.267
The approval is bound
to the exact payload,

00:01:11.267 --> 00:01:13.567
so what Sophia approves is what runs.

00:01:13.633 --> 00:01:15.100
Third, safe by default.

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:18.467
Dry Mode is an organization wide
switch that only a human can flip,

00:01:18.467 --> 00:01:21.867
and while it's on, every external
effect becomes a logged draft.

00:01:21.867 --> 00:01:25.700
Pro move: start there, and read what
would have happened before going live.

00:01:25.733 --> 00:01:28.167
Last, updates that never touch your data.

00:01:28.167 --> 00:01:31.200
An update only adds, and
starts with a verified backup.

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:33.767
A failed one rolls back
by swapping images,

00:01:33.767 --> 00:01:35.933
never by restoring over your database.

00:01:35.967 --> 00:01:40.367
Now one loop, end to end. Leo
connects the tools you already use.

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:43.667
Mia says who you sell to, in the
console or through the agent.

00:01:43.667 --> 00:01:45.600
It lands as the same definitions.

00:01:45.667 --> 00:01:48.967
The engines score accounts, fill in
what's missing, route to an owner,

00:01:48.967 --> 00:01:50.300
and draft the outreach.

00:01:50.333 --> 00:01:54.800
Orbit proposes a first touch. Sophia
reads the exact draft, and approves.

00:01:54.833 --> 00:01:57.600
Only then does it go out,
and the prospect receives it.

00:01:57.667 --> 00:02:01.300
Outcomes flow back, so the next score
starts from what actually happened.

00:02:01.367 --> 00:02:04.000
Underneath, your data is
separated per organization,

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:07.033
and the database itself refuses
cross organization reads.

00:02:07.033 --> 00:02:09.900
Every action lands in a
hash chained audit log.

00:02:09.967 --> 00:02:14.033
You may be thinking: an approval on
every touch will bury my team. It won't.

00:02:14.033 --> 00:02:16.700
A person can grant an agent
an envelope with a scope,

00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:18.033
a budget and an expiry.

00:02:18.033 --> 00:02:20.867
And the rule lives in the
operation, not in a prompt.

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:22.800
To recap: one place for your definitions,

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:26.000
agents that ask while a person
decides, and a safe default.

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:29.100
Agents move fast. Your
rules decide how far.

00:02:29.167 --> 00:02:33.300
This opens a series that follows the
order you'd build in: plan, build,

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:35.733
operate, automate, optimize, scale.

00:02:35.767 --> 00:02:39.567
So you can hand your go to market to
agents without handing over control.

00:02:39.567 --> 00:02:42.433
Open an empty folder. Next,
first setup with Claude.
